# Normalize text files to LF in the repository and working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Common source, docs, and generated data files.
*.cff text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
# Binary assets (never normalize or diff as text).
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.bz2 binary
*.sqlite binary
*.ifo binary
*.idx binary
*.dz binary

# --- control-character-dense document (H2173 follow-up, 04-08-2026) ---
# NUL bytes were stripped from both former offenders. AEK_et_al_corrected_2020.md then
# reclassified to `i/lf` and needs no exemption. This one did NOT: its garbled
# symbol-font table is dense with control bytes (\x08 \x10 \x11 \x15 \x1a), which git's
# binary heuristic reads as binary with or without NUL — so `--renormalize` still skips it
# and it would read as permanently dirty on every checkout. Declared binary so git's real
# behaviour is on the books. Fixing it properly means re-extracting the source PDF.
"literature/md/Lexicography-Manuals/THE ROUTLEDGE HANDBOOK OF HISTORICAL LINGUISTICS.md" binary
